    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/#built" class="crumb">Built with Mojibake</a></p>
    <h1>Check the citations. Inspect the evidence.</h1>
    <p class="study-product">LegalGrounding &middot; <a href="https://legalgrounding.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">legalgrounding.com&nbsp;&rarr;</a></p>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The task</h2>
      <p>
        A lawyer is about to file a brief. Somewhere in it are citations that came out of a model,
        or out of a hurry. One of them may not exist. Another may exist but stand for something
        other than what the brief says it does. A third may have been overruled last year.
        The judge will find out either way; the question is whether the lawyer finds out first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The information required</h2>
      <p>
        Not an opinion about the brief &mdash; the actual record. Whether each cited case exists.
        Whether the volume, reporter, page and year in the citation point at that case. What the
        case actually held, in its own words. And whether it is still good law, or has been
        overruled, abrogated or called into doubt since.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <p>
        LegalGrounding reads the brief, extracts every citation, and checks each one in four tiers
        through Mojibake&rsquo;s data layer: existence, then the citation tuple, then whether the
        quoted proposition is grounded in the opinion, then negative treatment. Every call routes
        through Pipeworx to CourtListener, so each verdict comes back attached to the document it
        was decided from rather than to a model&rsquo;s recollection of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block">
      <h2>The result</h2>
      <p>
        Live and self-serve, with four check tiers and a REST API. Evaluated across roughly
        <strong>4,760 citations in about 160 real federal filings</strong>. The
        <em>Mata&nbsp;v.&nbsp;Avianca</em> pattern &mdash; the brief that made fabricated citations
        a known failure &mdash; runs nightly as a regression test: <strong>six of six fabrications
        caught</strong>, with the genuine control citations left alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-block study-limits">
      <h2>What remains limited</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Not found is not the same as fabricated,</strong> and the product says so rather
        than guessing. A citation that cannot be matched may be unpublished, mis-transcribed, or
        simply outside the corpus &mdash; so it is reported as unverified, not as invented. Treating
        those as equivalent would produce exactly the confident wrong answer the tool exists to
        catch.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also barely marketed. The product works; getting it in front of the lawyers who need
        it is the open problem, not the checking.
      </p>
    </section>

